---
/**
 * Dean K. Phillips — the chapter's namesake.
 *
 * The legacy site had a page called "becoming a memorial chapter" that told the
 * story of restoring his plaque in 2006 and NEVER SAID WHO HE WAS. No rank
 * detail, no service history, no account of how the chapter came to bear his
 * name. The chapter's own identity was missing from its website.
 *
 * The biography below is transcribed from the bronze plaque the chapter itself
 * commissioned and installed at Fort Meade — a primary source, and the most
 * authoritative one available.
 */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import ReviewNote from '../../components/ReviewNote.astro';
import site from '../../data/site.json';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Captain Dean K. Phillips"
  description="The chapter is named for Captain Dean K. Phillips — 101st Airborne in Vietnam, author of the Vietnam Era Veterans' Readjustment Assistance Act, and an early force behind the Vietnam Veterans Memorial."
  eyebrow="About"
  lede="Chapter 227 is a memorial chapter. This is the man it remembers."
  image="/images/phillips-plaque.jpg"
>
  <div class="lede-block">
    <p class="dates">7 June 1943 &ndash; 22 August 1985</p>
  </div>

  <div class="split">
    <div class="split__text">
      <h2>Who he was</h2>
      <p>
        From December 1967 to November 1968, Dean K. Phillips served with bravery and distinction in the 101st
        Airborne Division in South Vietnam.
      </p>
      <p>
        Afterward, as a member of the Army Reserve 11th Special Forces Group at Fort Meade, he shared his combat
        experience and leadership with a new generation of soldiers.
      </p>
      <p>
        He became an early advocate for Vietnam veteran health and economic issues. He was responsible for the
        <strong> Vietnam Era Veterans&rsquo; Readjustment Assistance Act of 1974</strong>, and was instrumental in
        obtaining federal government approval for the <strong>Vietnam Veterans Memorial</strong> &mdash; The Wall
        that chapter members now gather at every Memorial Day.
      </p>
      <p class="source">
        Transcribed from the bronze plaque at the Captain Dean K. Phillips Parachute Drop Zone, Fort Meade,
        Maryland, commissioned by this chapter.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src="/images/phillips-plaque.jpg"
      alt="A bronze plaque set in stone reading: Captain Dean K. Phillips Parachute Drop Zone, born June 7 1943, died August 22 1985, followed by his service with the 101st Airborne Division and his work on veterans legislation and the Vietnam Veterans Memorial."
      caption="The plaque at the drop zone that bears his name."
      maxWidth="100%"
    />
  </div>

  <ReviewNote topic="How the chapter took his name">
    <p>
      Captain Phillips died on 22 August 1985, about four weeks after the fifteen founders met at the Fort Myer NCO
      Club on 24 July 1985. The current website never explains how or when the chapter chose to carry his name, or
      what connection its founders had to him.
    </p>
    <p>
      Someone in the chapter knows this story. It belongs on this page, in their words &mdash; it is the single most
      compelling piece of writing the chapter could publish, and it is currently nowhere.
    </p>
  </ReviewNote>

  <section class="stack">
    <h2>The drop zone, and the plaque</h2>
    <p>
      A former parachute drop zone at Fort Meade, Maryland is dedicated to Captain Phillips. The land is now part of
      the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service&rsquo;s Patuxent Research Refuge &mdash; North Tract.
    </p>
    <p>
      In response to questions about who Captain Phillips was, a park volunteer searched the internet and found the
      chapter&rsquo;s website. After an email inquiry, Len Ignatowski and Joe Celesnik went to inspect the drop zone
      and found a deteriorated plaque. The chapter voted to fund its renovation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Environmental and park regulations meant replacing the damaged, corroding original with a larger bronze
      plaque, carrying more personal information about the man it honored. Special thanks went to Alex Bigler, a
      retired Marine officer and owner of the National Trust Foundry, who took a personal interest in the
      inscription and the casting.
    </p>
    <p>
      Len Ignatowski and Joe Celesnik &mdash; both Vietnam War veterans &mdash; installed the new plaque on
      <strong> 26 August 2006</strong>. Diane Ignatowski and Sharon Celesnik of the chapter&rsquo;s AVVA, and Esther
      Woodworth, a refuge volunteer, were also there.
    </p>

    <Figure
      src="/images/phillips-plaque-installation.jpg"
      alt="Two men kneeling on either side of the newly installed Dean K. Phillips plaque beneath a tree at the drop zone, with a small American flag planted in the grass in front of it."
      caption="Len Ignatowski and Joe Celesnik at the newly installed plaque, 26 August 2006."
      credit="Photograph courtesy of the chapter."
    />

    <p>
      Ms. Helen Phillips, Captain Phillips&rsquo;s mother, telephoned Len Ignatowski to express her sincere
      appreciation for the new plaque, and for the photographs of it and of the memorial site that were sent to her.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>The plaque installation fulfills our commitment to &ldquo;{site.motto}.&rdquo;</p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .lede-block {
    margin-block-end: var(--s-6);
  }

  .dates {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--t-xl);
    color: var(--c-ink-faint);
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 0.9fr;
    align-items: start;
    gap: var(--s-7);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-block-end: var(--s-4);
  }

  p + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-4);
  }

  section {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-8);
  }

  section p,
  section :global(.figure) {
    max-width: var(--w-prose);
  }

  section :global(.figure) {
    margin-block: var(--s-6);
  }

  .source {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-5);
    padding-block-start: var(--s-4);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    color: var(--c-ink-faint);
  }

  blockquote {
    max-width: var(--w-prose);
    margin: var(--s-6) 0 0;
    padding-inline-start: var(--s-5);
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--c-gold);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--c-ink-soft);
  }

  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--s-5);
    }
  }
</style>
